Somatic Symptom Disorder
Mental Health & Substance Use
Distressing somatic symptoms, Disproportionate anxiety about symptom severity, Excessive time devoted to health concerns

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MHS-047
Reason for Visit: Severe, unremitting, non-specific abdominal and joint pain leading to total functional collapse and demanding immediate narcotic relief.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute crisis involving 10/10 diffuse pain, inability to mobilize, severe emotional distress, and repeated demands for an ambulance. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to definitively rule out an acute surgical abdomen or systemic inflammatory crisis, manage the acute distress, and safely prevent an unnecessary, iatrogenic emergency department admission prior to regular hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My entire body is in agony, my stomach is twisting, my joints are on fire. I'm dying and none of the doctors will listen to me. I need strong painkillers immediately."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports one or more somatic symptoms (diffuse abdominal and joint pain) that are extremely distressing and result in significant disruption of daily life. The spouse confirms a chronicity of the symptomatic state typically lasting more than six months. The patient exhibits disproportionate and persistent thoughts about the seriousness of their symptoms, harboring a persistently high level of anxiety about health. There is excessive time and energy devoted to these symptoms or health concerns, dominating all household conversation. The spouse notes frequent medical visits leading to extensive and often redundant diagnostic testing, all of which have been negative. The patient reports non-response to standard medical treatments for the reported symptoms, and displays extreme sensitivity to medication side effects. There is profound impairment in occupational and social functioning due to the physical complaints.
Functional Status: Currently bedbound, demanding high-level care from the spouse, unable to perform basic ADLs due to perceived pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe caregiver burnout; high risk of iatrogenic harm from over-investigation and unnecessary polypharmacy.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.9 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 80 Kg
General: Vocalizing pain continuously, shifting comfortably in bed when distracted, highly focused on describing medical history.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular and respiratory systems clear. Abdomen is soft, non-tender to deep palpation when the patient's attention is diverted, no guarding, no rebound. Bowel sounds present. Musculoskeletal exam reveals full, active range of motion in all major joints with normal strength, despite subjective reports of agonizing pain; no joint effusion, erythema, or warmth. Neurological exam intact. The clinical picture is dominated by distressing somatic symptoms, disproportionate anxiety about symptom severity, and excessive time devoted to health concerns, entirely out of proportion to the benign objective exam.
Environmental Observations: A massive binder of normal medical test results and imaging reports is on the bedside table.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient demands opiates. Currently prescribed Paracetamol/Ibuprofen, which they claim are "useless."

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Somatic Symptom Disorder - Status: Exacerbated (Acute somatic and behavioral crisis)
Differentials:

Factitious Disorder - Ruled out/unlikely because the patient is genuinely experiencing the subjective distress and pain; they are not consciously faking or intentionally inducing the symptoms for external or internal gain.

Acute Surgical Abdomen - Ruled out/unlikely due to the completely soft abdomen, absence of peritoneal signs, normal vital signs (no fever/shock), and a chronic history of identical, benign presentations.

Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE) - Ruled out/unlikely as the primary current acute driver, given the extensive history of negative diagnostic testing and the complete absence of objective inflammatory signs (effusions, rashes, fever) during the exam.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (Iatrogenic risk and caregiver burnout)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with subjective claims of excruciating, life-threatening pain demanding immediate, high-risk medical intervention (narcotics/ambulance). Telephone triage correctly prioritized the report of an acute abdomen. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was absolutely critical to conduct a thorough objective examination. Confirming the absence of acute pathology allowed the clinician to safely refuse the administration of dangerous narcotics and prevent a resource-intensive emergency department visit, which would only reinforce the patient's pathological anxiety and expose them to further iatrogenic harm. This safe diversion could only be achieved via in-person medical clearance.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Conducted a thorough, validating physical examination. Explicitly reassured the patient that there is no acute surgical emergency or life-threatening pathology present tonight. Firmly declined the prescription of opiate analgesia, explaining the risks of hyperalgesia and dependence. Expected investigation results (outpatient): repeated negative comprehensive medical investigations ruling out organic etiologies.
Positive Behavioral Change: Implement "Scheduled Checking." Advise the spouse to provide empathy for the patient's distress, but to limit conversations entirely focused on medical symptoms to a specific 15-minute window per day. Outside of that window, redirect the conversation to neutral topics. Reward the patient's participation in neutral topics with engaged, positive social interaction. This operant conditioning reduces the secondary gain (attention) driven by symptom reporting.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to a multidisciplinary pain clinic emphasizing psychological management.
DME/Safety (Optional): Nil.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 1 week to establish a single, consistent provider for scheduled, non-acute reviews.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department or call 000 only if objective, measurable new signs appear: e.g., vomiting blood, objective high fever (>38.5C), or a visibly hot, swollen, red joint.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Acute physical emergency ruled out; chronic pain and symptom distress require specialized psychological and rehabilitative management.
Clinical Course: The subjective pain is chronic and fluctuating. Narcotic medications will worsen the condition long-term.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If your stomach becomes hard like a board, or if you begin vomiting continuously and cannot keep fluids down, seek emergency medical care.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The pain you feel is absolutely real, but my physical exam confirms that there is no dangerous infection, blockage, or damage causing it right now. Your nervous system is amplifying normal signals into severe pain. Strong painkillers are dangerous for this type of nervous system pain.
Actionable Steps: Follow up with your regular GP to create a long-term care plan. Limit your medical research online. Focus on gentle, daily movement.

